{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_n4zd7fbhye3ba6vnxunzd76xvjzyuuktvamctygfbcxllk262lwa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 121-line translation unit exporting exactly one function — `uint16_t float2half(float)` — a branchless float32→float16 converter driven by two 512-entry lookup tables indexed by the input's top 9 bits (sign + exponent).\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- [x] **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` diff is empty. C exports `float2half`; Rust exports `float2half`. 0 missing symbols, 0 undefined non-libc symbols, `ldd -r` clean. The two tables are `static` in C (internal linkage) and are correctly *not* exported by either `.so`.\n- [x] **Phase B** — all 22 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass across randomized inputs (fixed seed).\n- [x] **Phase C** — all 12 `ERRORS.md` rows have passing differential tests.\n- [x] **Every feature combination** — `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]` and `src/` has no `cfg(feature)`, so one combination exists; `run_all.sh` enumerates it mechanically and runs the suite under both it and `--no-default-features`, in **both debug and release** (debug matters — it enables Rust's overflow/bounds panics that release omits). 38 tests × 4 configurations, all green.\n\n## The decisive result\n\nSince the function takes one `f32` and no other state, its entire input domain is the 2^32 bit patterns — so I ran a **truly exhaustive** differential sweep rather than settling for sampling: **all 4,294,967,296 inputs, C vs. Rust through both `.so` files, zero mismatches** (~3s at 1.4 G inputs/s across 16 threads). A second exhaustive pass validates the C `.so` against a model parsed out of `c_src/src/lib.c`'s own table literals, so the Rust is proven to implement the C tables exactly.\n\n## Notable C quirks confirmed replicated\n\nThe interesting finding from mapping the tables was that `m__shift[255]` and `m__shift[511]` are **13**, not 24 like the neighbouring saturating region — so NaN payloads propagate, and a NaN with a payload below `0x2000` **degenerates to Infinity**. The translation reproduces this rather than \"fixing\" it, along with truncation (not round-to-nearest), and negative underflow yielding `-0` (`0x8000`) not `+0`.\n\n## On test adequacy\n\nPassing tests can be vacuous, so I mutation-tested the suite: `mutation_check.py` injects 10 deliberate bugs into `src/lib.rs`, rebuilds, and requires failure each time (always restoring the original). **10/10 caught**, including the two subtlest — flipping `m__shift[255]` to 24 (the plausible \"NaN fix\") and adding a NaN special case — plus single-entry table corruptions and a round-to-nearest \"improvement\".\n\nTwo incidental notes: `cargo check` compiled clean with no errors or warnings from the start, so no translation fixes were needed — the divergence count was zero throughout. I added `translation/.cargo/config.toml` with `net.offline = true` because crates.io is blocked in this sandbox; `libloading 0.8.9` resolved from the local cargo cache. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified (only the sanctioned `build/` directory was added)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":584,"cache_read_input_tokens":121103,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":584,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2a89b93-baeb-4d84-83c9-f977b983fc86","uuid":"70c8a93d-ca33-49fe-a58b-b2b231584d3a","timestamp":"2026-08-26T21:45:44.615Z"}
